// .markdownlint-cli2.jsonc - markdown lint configuration for the LIFE400 documentation set.
//
// One of four mechanical integrity gates over docs/modernization/**. Three of the four operate
// today: this file checks markdown structure, .mlc-config.json checks link resolution, and a
// citation-resolution check confirms every [<path>:<locator>] reference points at a real path
// and line range. The fourth is planned rather than present - a root mkdocs.yml will check
// navigation completeness and internal links through `mkdocs build --strict` - and that file is
// not part of this checkpoint, so nothing below depends on it or on a site build existing.
// Structure is enforced by a gate that runs rather than asserted in review.
//
// Run with:  npx --yes markdownlint-cli2@0.23.2 "**/*.md"
//
// The tool version is pinned on the command line rather than in a manifest. LIFE400 is an
// ILE COBOL, ILE CL and DDS estate whose platform baseline is
// ILE COBOL V3R7 · OS/400 V4R2 · IBM AS/400 Model 9406 [README.md:L264]; a package.json would
// misrepresent that to every reader and tool inspecting the repository. Nothing configured here
// needs an npm install - no custom rules, no markdown-it plugins, no extra module paths.
//
// Every rule turned off below is justified by a finding measured against this repository's own
// hand-authored markdown using markdownlint-cli2 v0.23.2 (markdownlint v0.41.1). No rule is
// disabled speculatively: a rule turned off "just in case" would defeat the gate.
{
  // Schema URI for editor validation only. It names the same tool version used on the command
  // line, so an editor and the gate agree on which keys and rules are valid.
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DavidAnson/markdownlint-cli2/v0.23.2/schema/markdownlint-cli2-config-schema.json",

  "config": {
    // Every rule not named below keeps its default state. Three rules are disabled, each with a
    // measured cause recorded beside it, and two more are restated explicitly because the
    // documentation set relies on them being active. No rule is disabled speculatively.
    "default": true,

    // MD009/no-trailing-spaces - DISABLED, and the narrowest of the three exceptions in both
    // what it covers and why. It exists for exactly two bytes, neither of them authored here.
    // README.md's header block inherits a single trailing space on two lines
    // [README.md:L3], [README.md:L4], present in the file before this documentation work began.
    // The README update is confined to adding the Modernization and Documentation sections,
    // correcting eight superseded `.cble` filenames, and labelling eight unlabelled fences;
    // rewriting an unrelated byte of the entry-point document is outside that scope, and the
    // platform footer, product table, screen reference, build steps, business-rule summary and
    // Y2K notes are cited as authoritative by the new documents and must not shift underneath
    // those citations. So the bytes stay and the rule yields.
    //
    // Disabling is the only mechanism available, which was established by measurement rather
    // than assumed. MD009 exempts a line ending in exactly `br_spaces` spaces so it can serve as
    // a hard line break, but the exemption requires br_spaces >= 2: at the default of 2 the rule
    // reports "Expected: 0 or 2; Actual: 1", and at 1 or 0 it reports "Expected: 0; Actual: 1",
    // which is stricter still. No value of br_spaces admits a single trailing space. Per-file
    // scoping is not available either - markdownlint-cli2 resolves rule configuration from the
    // nearest configuration file, so exempting one file would mean adding a second
    // configuration file or embedding markdownlint-disable comments in README.md, and both are
    // themselves edits this work is not authorised to make.
    //
    // Measured on the repository's nine hand-authored markdown files: 2 findings, both in
    // README.md, both a single trailing space, and no others anywhere. The scope of what is
    // given up is therefore two known bytes rather than a class of defect - and every other
    // whitespace rule stays active to cover the rest, MD010/hard-tabs, MD012/no-multiple-blanks,
    // MD027/no-multiple-space-blockquote, MD030/list-marker-space, MD023/heading-start-left and
    // MD039/no-space-in-links among them.
    "MD009": false,

    // MD013/line-length - DISABLED. Every claim about the legacy system carries an inline
    // [<path>:<locator>] citation immediately after the claim, so lines legitimately exceed the
    // 80-character default - the two business-driver bullets of the entry-point document run to
    // 369 and 267 characters for that reason [README.md:L74], [README.md:L75]. Measured on
    // README.md: 19 findings, the longest line 459 characters [README.md:L100].
    // Raising the limit instead of disabling it would still be an arbitrary number and would
    // still fail that line.
    "MD013": false,

    // MD060/table-column-style - DISABLED. The repository's existing tables use a compact pipe
    // style with no padding inside the delimiter row [README.md:L225], and the new documents
    // follow it so field inventories, column mappings and risk registers read consistently.
    // Measured on README.md: 30 findings across its four tables, whose delimiter rows are
    // [README.md:L14], [README.md:L63], [README.md:L82] and [README.md:L225]. Twenty-four of the
    // findings are reported against the delimiter row, for three of the four tables; the remaining
    // six are the body rows of the reading-paths table added to the Modernization section
    // [README.md:L83], [README.md:L84], because that is where the padding differs. Measured across
    // docs/**: 460 findings, so the rule is load-bearing for the authored set and not only
    // for the pre-existing overview. In this tool version the rule that reports compact table
    // pipes is MD060; MD055/table-pipe-style and
    // MD056/table-column-count stay at their defaults because neither fires here.
    "MD060": false,

    // MD040/fenced-code-language - ENFORCED. Restated rather than left implicit because the
    // documentation set depends on it: every fenced block names its language, `mermaid` for each
    // diagram under docs/modernization/**, and `text` or `bash` for layout and command blocks.
    // This rule is why README.md's eight formerly unlabelled fences now name theirs: they carry
    // `text` and sit at [README.md:L26], [README.md:L124], [README.md:L139], [README.md:L148],
    // [README.md:L159], [README.md:L181], [README.md:L197] and [README.md:L216]. The four `bash`
    // fences added with the Documentation section [README.md:L96], [README.md:L102],
    // [README.md:L108], [README.md:L114] were labelled when written, so all twelve opening fences
    // in the file name a language. Measured on README.md after that correction: 0 findings.
    "MD040": true,

    // MD041/first-line-heading - ENFORCED, restated for the same reason. Every document opens
    // with a single top-level heading, so a reader entering the set at any file sees that file's
    // subject on its first line.
    "MD041": true
  },

  // Paths excluded from linting. docs/** and README.md are deliberately absent from this list:
  // they are exactly what the gate exists to check.
  "ignores": [
    // Machine-generated Swimm corpus, consumed strictly as read-only reference. Its front
    // matter, <SwmPath> and <SwmToken> tags, repeated top-level headings and generated long
    // table rows are tool-emitted and cannot be hand-authored correctly. One 291-line
    // walkthrough alone reports 75 findings, most of them from rules this configuration keeps
    // enabled, so excluding the corpus both keeps the report readable and removes any temptation
    // to hand-correct a generator artifact.
    ".swm/**",

    // Output of `mkdocs build`, once the planned root mkdocs.yml makes a site build possible. A
    // local artifact, never linted and never committed.
    "site/**",

    // Defensive: the lint glob is "**/*.md", so any transient npm install would otherwise fill
    // the report with third-party markdown.
    "node_modules/**",

    // Defensive, same reasoning, for the Python side. This host's system Python carries a
    // PEP 668 externally-managed marker, which is why the install command README.md documents
    // above the lint command [README.md:L97] creates and activates a virtual environment before
    // installing. A repository-root environment installs mkdocs and mkdocs-material,
    // whose distributions ship 9 markdown files of their own; without these entries the
    // documented lint command reports MD040 and MD041 findings against third-party licence
    // text and the gate stops being usable. Measured with mkdocs 1.6.1 and mkdocs-material
    // 9.7.7 installed from requirements-docs.txt. The site-packages pattern is the catch-all,
    // so an environment under any directory name is covered; the two conventional names are
    // listed as well so the intent is readable without knowing the layout.
    "**/site-packages/**",
    ".venv/**",
    "venv/**"
  ]

  // Deliberately absent:
  //   "fix"    - the gate reports, it never silently rewrites. A tool that edits files is
  //              incompatible with the guarantee that no COBOL, CL, copybook, DDS or Swimm
  //              member is modified by this documentation work.
  //   "globs"  - the file list comes from the command line. QCBLLESRC/, QCLSRC/, QCPYSRC/ and
  //              QDDSSRC/ hold ILE COBOL, ILE CL, copybook and DDS members, contain no markdown,
  //              and are out of scope entirely.
  //   link validation - owned by .mlc-config.json today, and additionally by
  //              `mkdocs build --strict` once the planned root mkdocs.yml is added. Never by
  //              this file.
}
